# Rule exemptions scoped to a single file, rather than a global skip_list entry
# that would also silence the rule where it is right.
#
# Molecule verification playbooks read facts back off the machine — `systemctl
# show -p Result`, `systemctl list-timers`, `systemctl --failed`. systemd_service
# does return those pairs in its `status`, but it captures them BEFORE it acts
# and never refreshes them: measured, the module started a stopped unit, said
# `changed: true`, and reported `ActiveState=inactive` while the machine already
# said `active`. A test built on that reads the state a run was supposed to
# change, not the state it produced.
#
# The measurement, and how to repeat it, are in
# wiki/research/systemd-service-status-is-stale.md.
#
# Listing each file here keeps the warnings visible and marked `# ignored`
# rather than silencing them: a global skip_list would also mute the rule in
# roles/, where preferring the module is correct. A count is not given on
# purpose — it would need updating with every scenario, and a stale number in a
# comment about honesty is a poor look.
molecule/common/verify.yml command-instead-of-module
molecule/cron/verify.yml command-instead-of-module
molecule/fail2ban/verify.yml command-instead-of-module
molecule/firewall/verify.yml command-instead-of-module
molecule/firewall_cloudflare/verify.yml command-instead-of-module
molecule/nginx/verify.yml command-instead-of-module
molecule/nginx_auth/verify.yml command-instead-of-module
molecule/php/verify.yml command-instead-of-module
molecule/postgresql/verify.yml command-instead-of-module
